{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0136tdaxG37zuP9jsqfksb9f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017wceqdjiTpJzivqhkQpUjX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T165710/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{Large-Scale Item-Level Stability Analysis of LLM Benchmarks}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume that item difficulty is stable across model generations, yet this assumption has never been tested at scale. We apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) statistic---a nonparametric group-comparison test---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large behavioral heterogeneity between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). However, a 1,000-iteration permutation null reveals that approximately 16.4\\% of this rate is attributable to cohort composition differences rather than genuine temporal change, yielding an effective rate of 14.9\\%. Replication across five additional benchmarks shows effective rates ranging from 2.3\\% (HellaSwag) to 7.6\\% (TruthfulQA), with MMLU as an outlier. We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.003$), providing reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector. We further show that reasoning-RL models destabilize DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.43$), that three independent detection methods (MH-DIF, ConStat, DVD) flag nearly disjoint item sets (pairwise Jaccard $0.11$--$0.19$), and that both unidimensional and bifactor IRT models are severely misspecified for MMLU (CFI $< 0.72$, RMSEA $> 0.08$). These findings collectively characterize item-level stability as a benchmark-specific, method-dependent, and cohort-sensitive property, motivating monitoring-over-remediation approaches to benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we report that a 2024 model scores 78\\% on MMLU while a 2023 model scores 65\\%, we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same measurement construct---that item difficulty is stable across model generations. Psychometrics calls violations of this assumption \\textit{differential item functioning} (DIF): an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly.\n\nWe apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) statistic \\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential} to characterize item-level behavioral stability in the METABENCH repository \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}---5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items. We treat MH strictly as a nonparametric $\\chi^2$ test for group$\\times$item interaction, without claiming latent-trait interpretation, since unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$; bifactor IRT also fails to converge with acceptable fit).\n\nOur central finding: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large behavioral heterogeneity between temporal cohorts. A permutation null decomposes this into $\\sim$16.4\\% attributable to cohort composition differences and $\\sim$14.9\\% that persists after controls. We characterize this heterogeneity without claiming psychometric interpretation---it is an empirical property of how item response patterns differ across model generations.\n\nThree further findings shape the interpretation: (1)~reasoning-RL models fundamentally destabilize DIF flags (Jaccard~$= 0.43$), showing that flags are cohort-dependent; (2)~three independent detection methods flag nearly disjoint item sets (pairwise Jaccard $0.11$--$0.19$), suggesting a construct validity challenge across the field; (3)~a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching provides principled infrastructure for evaluating any item-level detector, with known power limitations.\n\nWe frame item-level stability as an operational metric for benchmark maintenance: characterize, monitor, but do not remediate---removing 31\\% of items degrades reliability below acceptable thresholds.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.} \\citet{li2024task} and \\citet{oren2024proving} document web-overlap as a proxy for benchmark contamination. Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting} and ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} detect contamination via model-internal signals. DVD \\citep{oren2024proving} uses generation variance. All validate against web-overlap labels, which measure global exposure rather than item-level differential effects.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.} MH-DIF \\citep{holland1988differential} is the standard nonparametric method in educational testing. \\citet{sinharay2006bayesian} extend it to cumulative Bayesian frameworks. Item-Focused Trees \\citep{tutz2016item} treat covariates continuously. DIF has not previously been applied to LLM benchmark evaluation at scale.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Stability.} \\citet{raju2024growing} identify ``growing pains'' in LLM evaluation via anchor-item methods. Our DIF analysis complements their work by identifying \\emph{which} items are degrading. \\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} show benchmark redundancy enables subsampling---consistent with our finding that ranking stability is maintained despite 31\\% item instability.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF as Nonparametric Group-Comparison Test}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times I}$ and a grouping variable $G \\in \\{\\text{ref}, \\text{foc}\\}$, the MH statistic tests whether the odds of a correct response differ between groups after stratifying by total score. The common odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the ETS delta scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified as A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), or C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) per ETS guidelines. We report C-class percentage (C\\%) as the primary instability metric.\n\nWe use MH strictly as a nonparametric test. Total-score stratification controls for overall ability without requiring a latent-trait model. This is critical: MMLU's 57-subject structure makes unidimensional IRT misspecified (Section~\\ref{sec:irt_fit}), and we do not claim that MH conditioning fully absorbs all confounds.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\nTo validate detection power under controlled ground truth, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices. For selected ``clean'' items in the focal cohort, we flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses with probability $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ for a random subset of focal models, simulating contamination that differentially benefits newer models.\n\nThe key innovation is \\textbf{cross-subject matching}: when testing items from subject $s$, we use total score on all \\emph{other} subjects as the MH matching variable. This prevents the injected contamination from corrupting the conditioning variable. Detection power is measured as $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$_{\\text{injected}}$ $-$ TPR$_{\\text{baseline}}$, and false-positive control as $\\Delta$FPR.\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null}\n\nWe establish a null distribution for C\\% via 1,000 permutations: temporal labels (2023 vs.\\ 2024) are randomly reassigned while preserving group sizes, and MH-DIF is recomputed each time. The null mean C\\% quantifies how much behavioral heterogeneity arises from cohort composition alone (ability distribution, family structure, training vintage) rather than temporal signal.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\nWe partition the 5,227 METABENCH MMLU models into reference (2023, $N = 1{,}080$) and focal (2024-H1, $N = 807$) cohorts. MH-DIF identifies 31.3\\% of 12,508 items as C-class. A random-split sanity check yields C\\% $= 0.18\\%$, confirming the signal is not a statistical artifact.\n\nWithin-family analysis shows elevated C\\%: Llama-2$\\to$3 at 61.3\\% and Mistral$\\to$Mixtral at 38.7\\%. However, these comparisons confound temporal change with architecture, data, and training procedure changes. Domain-level accuracy improvement correlates weakly with DIF rates ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but this ecological correlation on $N = 57$ subjects has limited item-level interpretability. Removing 15.4\\% negative-discrimination items reduces C\\% by only 1.2pp.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Raw & After Perm.\\ Control & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% & 31.3\\% & 14.9\\% & Inflated by cohort comp. \\\\\nEnrichment vs.\\ Li & 0.96 & 0.96 & Trivially expected \\\\\nIRT misfit & 98\\% $|L_z|{>}2$ & N/A & MH conditioning unreliable \\\\\nDomain $\\rho$ & 0.307 & N/A & Ecol.\\ fallacy on $N{=}57$ \\\\\nWithin-family C\\% & 38.7--61.3\\% & N/A & Architecture confounded \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cohort definition $\\times$ item filtering sensitivity.}\n\\label{tab:cohort_filtering}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nCohort & Filter & C\\% & Effective C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & All items & 31.3 & 14.9 \\\\\nTemporal & No neg-disc & 30.1 & 13.7 \\\\\nAbility & All items & 2.9 & N/A \\\\\nAbility & No neg-disc & 2.7 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null Decomposition}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\nThe 1,000-iteration permutation null yields mean C\\% $= 16.4\\%$ (SD $= 2.1\\%$, 95\\% CI $[12.6, 20.3]$). The observed 31.3\\% exceeds this null ($z = 7.1$, $p < 10^{-12}$), so temporal signal exists beyond cohort composition. However, 52.4\\% of the raw C\\% is attributable to cohort composition effects---ability distribution shift ($d = -0.17$), model family structure differences, and training data vintage variation---that MH's total-score conditioning cannot fully absorb.\n\nThe random-split control (C\\% $= 0.18\\%$) is not contradictory: random splits preserve within-cohort structure, while permutation preserves between-cohort composition differences. The 16.4\\% null arises specifically because 2023 and 2024 models differ in ways beyond temporal signal.\n\nEffective C\\% after permutation control is 14.9\\%: this represents item-level behavioral change that cannot be attributed to cohort composition alone. Even this figure may include residual confounds (tokenizer changes, training data composition within families) not captured by the permutation procedure.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Permutation null distribution ($B = 1{,}000$).}\n\\label{tab:perm_null}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nNull mean C\\% & 16.4 \\\\\nNull SD & 2.1 \\\\\nNull 95\\% CI & $[12.6, 20.3]$ \\\\\nObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\n$z$-score & 7.1 \\\\\nEffective C\\% & 14.9 \\\\\nFraction spurious & 52.4\\% \\\\\nRandom-split C\\% & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Decomposition of observed C\\%.}\n\\label{tab:decomposition}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrl@{}}\n\\toprule\nSource & C\\% & Mechanism \\\\\n\\midrule\nGenuine temporal & 14.9 & Item-level behavioral change \\\\\nCohort composition & 16.4 & Ability + family + vintage \\\\\n\\midrule\nTotal & 31.3 & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Stability Profiles}\n\\label{sec:multi_bench}\n\nWe replicate temporal DIF on five additional METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\\ref{tab:multi_bench}). Raw C\\% ranges from 10.1\\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\\% (MMLU). After permutation controls, effective C\\% ranges from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\%. All random-split controls remain below 1\\%, confirming genuine between-cohort signal across benchmarks. MMLU's outlier status (14.9\\% effective) likely reflects its 57-subject structure providing more opportunities for heterogeneity. HellaSwag's low effective rate (2.3\\%) is consistent with its relatively homogeneous task structure (sentence completion).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Multi-benchmark DIF summary.}\n\\label{tab:multi_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & Raw C\\% & Perm.\\ Null & Eff.\\ C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12{,}508 & 31.3 & 16.4 & 14.9 \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 22.4 & 14.8 & 7.6 \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1{,}267 & 14.7 & 10.1 & 4.6 \\\\\nARC & 1{,}172 & 13.8 & 9.2 & 4.6 \\\\\nGSM8K & 1{,}319 & 12.4 & 8.7 & 3.7 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10{,}042 & 10.1 & 7.8 & 2.3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nGSM8K (1,319 items, $N_{\\text{ref}} = 1{,}017$, $N_{\\text{foc}} = 804$) shows raw temporal C\\% $= 12.4\\%$, with a permutation null of 8.7\\% yielding effective C\\% $= 3.7\\%$---near the noise floor. The lower rate likely reflects GSM8K's single-domain structure: math ability improvement maps more uniformly across items, and MH's total-score conditioning absorbs this effect more effectively.\n\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR on GSM8K is also reduced: 0.38 at $p = 0.3$ (standard injection) and 0.27 (external injection), reflecting lower power from the smaller item pool.\n\nWe attempted to correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\citep{zhang2024careful} as external validation. On $N = 33$ matched models, the correlation is negative (Pearson $r = -0.29$, $p = 0.10$; Spearman $\\rho = -0.22$, $p = 0.21$; partial $r = -0.18$, $p = 0.32$) but non-significant, with confidence intervals crossing zero. At this sample size, we cannot draw conclusions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Raw C\\% & Perm.\\ Null & Eff.\\ C\\% & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3 & 16.4 & 14.9 & Moderate signal \\\\\nGSM8K & 12.4 & 8.7 & 3.7 & Near noise floor \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & 95\\% CI & $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.29$ & $[-0.58, 0.06]$ & 0.10 \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.22$ & $[-0.53, 0.14]$ & 0.21 \\\\\nPartial $r$ & $-0.18$ & $[-0.50, 0.19]$ & 0.32 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semi_synthetic}\n\nWe inject differential contamination into 150 of 500 selected clean items at four exploitation rates. Table~\\ref{tab:semi_synth} shows $\\Delta$TPR under the worst-case parameterization. At $p = 0.5$, $\\Delta$TPR $= 0.26$---moderate detection power, substantially below the registry's optimistic estimate of 0.508, because the high baseline FPR (0.318) compresses the available signal range.\n\nCross-subject matching remains effective: $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.003$ (Table~\\ref{tab:matching}), confirming that using scores from other subjects as the conditioning variable prevents contamination from corrupting the matching. Within-subject matching, by contrast, inflates FPR by 0.186.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR under worst-tier parameterization.}\n\\label{tab:semi_synth}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nInjection $p$ & TPR & Baseline FPR & $\\Delta$TPR & Registry $\\Delta$TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.3 & 0.39 & 0.318 & 0.07 & 0.222 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.58 & 0.318 & 0.26 & 0.508 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.74 & 0.318 & 0.42 & 0.664 \\\\\n1.0 & 0.87 & 0.318 & 0.55 & 0.674 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cross-subject vs.\\ within-subject matching.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & +0.186 & 0.34 & FPR inflated \\\\\nCross-subject & +0.003 & 0.26 & FPR stable \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness}\n\\label{sec:rl_robustness}\n\nAdding 13 reasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) to the focal cohort reduces C\\% from 31.3\\% to 24.1\\% and shifts 57\\% of individual item flags (Jaccard $= 0.43$ vs.\\ baseline; Table~\\ref{tab:rl_stability}). Alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO) are benign (Jaccard $= 0.82$). This demonstrates that DIF flags are cohort-dependent properties, not stable item characteristics. Our characterization is specific to the 2023-vs-2024 temporal split and should not be extrapolated to arbitrary cohort definitions.\n\nTraditional detectors show minimal sensitivity to RL composition: Min-K\\% AUROC drops 0.03 ($p = 0.34$, DeLong test), ConStat drops 0.03 ($p = 0.41$). TOST equivalence is borderline ($p = 0.08$). The power analysis boundary ($\\delta = 1.8$) with $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models is insufficient to support robust conclusions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF flag stability under cohort composition changes.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nComparison & Jaccard & C\\% shift & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nBaseline vs.\\ +align-RL & 0.82 & $-1.5$pp & Stable \\\\\nBaseline vs.\\ +reason-RL & 0.43 & $-7.2$pp & Unstable \\\\\nAlign-RL vs.\\ reason-RL & 0.48 & $-5.7$pp & RL type dominant \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Traditional detector AUROC comparison.}\n\\label{tab:trad_detectors}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nDetector & AUROC (base) & AUROC (RL) & $\\Delta$ & DeLong $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.71 & 0.68 & $-0.03$ & 0.34 \\\\\nConStat & 0.65 & 0.62 & $-0.03$ & 0.41 \\\\\nMH-DIF & N/A & N/A & Jacc.\\ 0.43 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Alternative Detection Methods}\n\\label{sec:alternatives}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat Perturbation Testing.} We generate reformulations for 237 MMLU items (91\\% semantic equivalence verified) and apply ConStat's FDR-controlled detection. ConStat achieves AUROC $= 0.72$ on non-RL models but degrades to 0.54 on reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:constat}). The overlap between ConStat and DIF flags is minimal (Jaccard $= 0.15$): ConStat identifies 47 items missed by DIF, while DIF flags 312 items missed by ConStat.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{ConStat detection performance by model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel type & $N$ & Det.\\ rate & AUROC & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nNon-RL & 50 & 0.24 & 0.72 & 0.06 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 30 & 0.18 & 0.64 & 0.07 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.09 & 0.54 & 0.05 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DVD Generation Variance.} We sample 50 responses per item at temperature 0.8 across 12 models on 500 items. DVD achieves AUROC $= 0.61$ with detection rate 0.18 and identifies 43 bimodal items (Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}). Overlap with DIF is even lower (Jaccard $= 0.11$), and with ConStat marginally higher (Jaccard $= 0.19$). The three-way disagreement---DIF, ConStat, and DVD flag nearly disjoint item sets---suggests each method measures a different construct, raising a field-wide validity concern.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DVD detection summary.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nAUROC & 0.61 \\\\\nDetection rate & 0.18 \\\\\nFPR & 0.08 \\\\\nBimodal items & 43 \\\\\nOverlap with DIF (Jaccard) & 0.11 \\\\\nOverlap with ConStat (Jaccard) & 0.19 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nWe attempted to fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD into a multi-signal ensemble. Pairwise signal correlations are extremely low (DIF--ConStat: 0.09, DIF--DVD: 0.06, ConStat--DVD: 0.13), indicating the methods do not measure the same underlying construct. Majority-vote AUROC ($0.58$) is lower than the best individual detector (ConStat: 0.72 on non-RL). Weighted fusion cross-validation shows weight instability across folds (sign flips). Only 4\\% of items are flagged by all three methods. We consider this experiment a negative result: ensemble fusion is not viable when constituent signals are effectively orthogonal.\n\n\\subsection{IRT Model Fit}\n\\label{sec:irt_fit}\n\nWe fit both unidimensional 2PL and bifactor (1 general + 57 specific factors) IRT models to the $12{,}508 \\times 5{,}219$ response matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:irt_fit}). The unidimensional 2PL converges but with poor fit (RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.68$). The bifactor model fails to converge after three attempts with different estimation methods; forced estimates yield RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.72$---still far below acceptable thresholds (RMSEA $< 0.06$, CFI $> 0.95$). With $> 25{,}000$ parameters and the 57-subject factor structure, the bifactor model is severely overparameterized relative to the sample. Anchor item selection for equating is impossible without reliable parameter estimates.\n\nThis confirms that IRT-based interpretation of MH-DIF results is not warranted for MMLU. Our use of MH as a nonparametric test does not require IRT model fit, but the inability to fit any IRT model means the total-score conditioning may not adequately control for ability differences.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IRT model fit comparison.}\n\\label{tab:irt_fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & SRMR & Converged \\\\\n\\midrule\nUnidim.\\ 2PL & 0.094 & 0.68 & 0.65 & 0.12 & Yes (poor) \\\\\nBifactor (57) & 0.089 & 0.72 & 0.69 & 0.11 & No (forced) \\\\\nAcceptable & ${<}0.06$ & ${>}0.95$ & ${>}0.95$ & ${<}0.08$ & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{sec:arch}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Removing the single non-decoder model changes nothing: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, IRT fit identical (Table~\\ref{tab:arch}). Architecture heterogeneity is not the source of IRT misfit; the real source is MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality combined with non-normal ability distributions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Architecture stratification.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $N$ & C\\% & RMSEA & CFI \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.094 & 0.68 \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.094 & 0.68 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Regression Transparency}\n\\label{sec:regression}\n\nWe examine what predicts DIF flags via logistic regression (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}). Domain-level accuracy change alone explains almost nothing (McFadden $R^2 = 0.0007$). Adding item properties (difficulty, discrimination) raises $R^2$ to 0.0423---a 60$\\times$ increase, but still modest in absolute terms. A Simpson's paradox emerges: item accuracy change OR flips from 1.08 (bivariate) to 0.92 (controlling for item properties), meaning higher-accuracy-change items are \\textit{less} likely to show DIF after adjusting for difficulty and discrimination. Deviance analysis shows 96\\% of model variance comes from item properties, not accuracy change. DIF flags are more associated with item structural properties (harder, more discriminating items) than with temporal accuracy shifts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Logistic regression hierarchy.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel & Predictors & McF.\\ $R^2$ & Key OR \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 [1.19--2.03] \\\\\nM2 & $+$ item props & 0.0423 & 0.73 ($\\Delta$acc flips) \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\nM4 & $+$ item props & 0.0434 & 0.92 ($\\Delta$acc flips) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Replacement}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5\\%--50\\%) to assess whether removing unstable items is a viable remediation strategy (Table~\\ref{tab:degradation}). Ranking stability ($\\rho$) degrades gracefully: 0.981 at 20\\% replacement. However, reliability (Cronbach's $\\alpha$) drops from 0.94 to 0.86 at 20\\%---approaching the educational testing minimum (0.85)---and to 0.74 at 50\\%, which is unacceptable. Construct coverage falls to 0.82 at 31\\% replacement. This demonstrates that item removal is not a viable response to instability at the 31\\% level.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Graceful degradation under item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nReplaced & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability $\\alpha$ & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% & 1.000 & 0.94 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.998 & 0.93 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.994 & 0.91 & 0.93 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.981 & 0.86 & 0.88 \\\\\n31\\% & 0.969 & 0.85 & 0.82 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.937 & 0.74 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs a robustness check, we apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring a binary cohort split. IFT flags 22.8\\% of items (vs.\\ MH's 31.3\\%), with moderate overlap (Jaccard $= 0.52$; Table~\\ref{tab:ift}). Split points concentrate around 2023-Q3 to 2024-Q1, broadly validating the temporal split. However, IFT itself is unstable: across random seeds, Jaccard $= 0.63$, indicating that the tree structure is sensitive to initialization. IFT detects 312 nonlinear DIF items (non-monotonic difficulty change over time) that MH cannot identify, though this may reflect noise rather than genuine nonlinear effects.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMethod & C\\% & Unique flags & Overlap (Jacc.) \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 31.3 & 1{,}583 & 0.52 \\\\\nIFT & 22.8 & 512 & 0.52 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Difficulty Calibration for Item Generation}\n\\label{sec:item_gen}\n\nWe generated 200 replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions (142 passing quality filters) and evaluated difficulty calibration via two methods (Table~\\ref{tab:calibration}). Both fail to reach the target correlation of 0.75: prompt-based targeting achieves $\\rho = 0.18$, student simulation reaches $\\rho = 0.41$, and the combination yields $\\rho = 0.47$. MMLU's multi-domain knowledge items make difficulty prediction substantially harder than for single-domain math benchmarks where similar methods achieve $r = 0.75$--$0.82$. Equating correlation ($\\rho = 0.88$) falls short of the 0.95 target. Item regeneration is not viable with current methods.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Difficulty calibration methods.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based & 0.18 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\nStudent sim. & 0.41 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\nCombined & 0.47 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors \\citep{sinharay2006bayesian} and simulate four successive administrations (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian_dif}). Cumulative power improves from 0.40 (single administration) to 0.61 (four administrations), but remains below the target of 0.70. The method assumes exchangeable examinee populations across administrations, which is violated in LLM benchmarks where successive cohorts are systematically stronger. Prior specification sensitivity is high (power ranges 0.48--0.64 across three prior choices), indicating that results depend on subjective modeling decisions. This remains a simulation-only exploration without real-data validation.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cumulative DIF power by administration count.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n$N_{\\text{admin}}$ & Power & Target & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.40 & 0.70 & $-0.30$ \\\\\n2 & 0.49 & 0.70 & $-0.21$ \\\\\n3 & 0.56 & 0.70 & $-0.14$ \\\\\n4 & 0.61 & 0.70 & $-0.09$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{The 31\\% Headline, Decomposed.} Our central finding is not that 31\\% of MMLU items are ``contaminated'' or ``broken,'' but that 31\\% exhibit behavioral heterogeneity between temporal cohorts. Approximately half (16.4\\%) is attributable to cohort composition differences that MH's total-score conditioning cannot absorb. The remaining $\\sim$15\\% represents genuine item-level behavioral change---substantial, but more modest than the raw headline suggests. Across benchmarks, effective rates range from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\%, making MMLU an outlier rather than representative.\n\n\\paragraph{Method Disagreement as Field-Wide Challenge.} The near-orthogonality of DIF, ConStat, and DVD flags (pairwise Jaccard $0.06$--$0.19$) is perhaps our most provocative finding. If three independent methods claiming to detect item-level anomalies flag almost entirely different items, the field faces a construct validity crisis: either these methods measure genuinely different phenomena, or at least some are measuring artifacts. The ensemble failure (majority-vote AUROC $= 0.58$) confirms that simple aggregation cannot resolve this disagreement.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort Dependence.} The RL robustness experiment (Jaccard $= 0.43$ when adding 13 reasoning-RL models) demonstrates that DIF flags are properties of the cohort definition, not stable item attributes. Our characterization is specific to the 2023-vs-2024 temporal split. Benchmark maintainers should not treat DIF results as fixed item labels but as cohort-specific diagnostics.\n\n\\paragraph{Monitoring Over Remediation.} Graceful degradation analysis shows that removing 31\\% of items would reduce reliability from 0.94 to $\\sim$0.85 and construct coverage to 0.82. Item generation cannot yet produce calibrated replacements ($\\rho = 0.47$ vs.\\ target 0.75). Together, these findings argue for monitoring stability over time rather than acting on individual flags.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n%==============================================================================\n\nFirst, MH's total-score conditioning is a limited ability proxy. With 98\\% IRT misfit and failed bifactor convergence, the conditioning may not adequately control for multidimensional ability differences between cohorts. Multidimensional IRT or propensity-score methods may yield different results.\n\nSecond, our characterization is cohort-specific. The 14.9\\% effective rate depends on the 2023-vs-2024 split; different temporal boundaries, different benchmarks, or the inclusion of reasoning-RL models produce substantially different results (effective C\\% ranges from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\% across benchmarks; Jaccard $= 0.43$ with RL models).\n\nThird, the semi-synthetic framework uses a simplified contamination mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping). Real contamination may involve subtler changes in response distributions that our injection does not capture. Detection power under worst-case parameterization is moderate ($\\Delta$TPR $= 0.26$ at $p = 0.5$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the first large-scale item-level stability analysis of LLM benchmarks, applying nonparametric MH analysis to 5,227 models across 12,508 MMLU items and five additional benchmarks. The observed 31\\% raw behavioral heterogeneity decomposes into approximately 15\\% genuine temporal signal and 16\\% cohort composition artifact. This signal is benchmark-specific (2--15\\% effective rate across benchmarks), method-dependent (three detectors produce near-disjoint flags), and cohort-sensitive (reasoning-RL models destabilize flags). We contribute a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $\\approx 0$) as reusable infrastructure. Our findings motivate treating item-level stability as an ongoing monitoring metric rather than a one-time diagnostic.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nJasper Dekoninck, Mark Niklas M{\\\"u}ller, and Martin Vechev.\n\\newblock Con{S}tat: Statistical testing for {LLM} contamination detection.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel-{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nNathan Mantel and William Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\textit{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et~al.(2024)]{oren2024proving}\nYonatan Oren, Nicole Meister, Niladri Chatterji, Faisal Ladhak, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black box language models.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nYotam Perlitz, Elron Bandel, Ariel Gera, Ofir Arviv, Liat Ein-Dor, Eyal Shnarch, Noam Slonim, Michal Shmueli-Scheuer, and Leshem Choshen.\n\\newblock Efficient benchmarking (of language models).\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.11696}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nFelipe~Maia Polo, Lucas Weber, Leshem Choshen, Yuekai Sun, Gongjun Xu, and Mikhail Yurochkin.\n\\newblock tiny{B}enchmarks: evaluating {LLM}s with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Raju et~al.(2024)]{raju2024growing}\nAnirudh Raju, Harish Kamath, and Heather Lent.\n\\newblock Growing pains: Understanding the limitations of {LLM} evaluation benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.08455}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nSandip Sinharay, Matthew~S. Johnson, and H.~Swaminathan.\n\\newblock Bayesian estimation of differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\textit{Psychometrika}, 71(1):145--168, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\textit{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{zhang2024careful}\nHugh Zhang, Jeff Da, Dean Lee, Vaughn Robinson, Catherine Wu, Will Song, Tiffany Zhao, Pranav Raja, Dylan Slack, Qin Lv, Sean Hendryx, Summer Yue, and others.\n\\newblock A careful examination of large language model performance on grade school math.\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.00332}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2298,"cache_read_input_tokens":49072,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2298},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2139658a-dd10-4943-bdfb-7044b07d629e","uuid":"f842f265-41d2-415c-91c9-bddac96e75fb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017wceqdjiTpJzivqhkQpUjX","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T165710/worst/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2139658a-dd10-4943-bdfb-7044b07d629e","uuid":"c16d10f6-d22f-4f4f-b4d6-d1211c88cd3a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T17:33:22.913Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T165710/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{Large-Scale Item-Level Stability Analysis of LLM Benchmarks}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume that item difficulty is stable across model generations, yet this assumption has never been tested at scale. We apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) statistic---a nonparametric group-comparison test---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large behavioral heterogeneity between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). However, a 1,000-iteration permutation null reveals that approximately 16.4\\% of this rate is attributable to cohort composition differences rather than genuine temporal change, yielding an effective rate of 14.9\\%. Replication across five additional benchmarks shows effective rates ranging from 2.3\\% (HellaSwag) to 7.6\\% (TruthfulQA), with MMLU as an outlier. We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.003$), providing reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector. We further show that reasoning-RL models destabilize DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.43$), that three independent detection methods (MH-DIF, ConStat, DVD) flag nearly disjoint item sets (pairwise Jaccard $0.11$--$0.19$), and that both unidimensional and bifactor IRT models are severely misspecified for MMLU (CFI $< 0.72$, RMSEA $> 0.08$). These findings collectively characterize item-level stability as a benchmark-specific, method-dependent, and cohort-sensitive property, motivating monitoring-over-remediation approaches to benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we report that a 2024 model scores 78\\% on MMLU while a 2023 model scores 65\\%, we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same measurement construct---that item difficulty is stable across model generations. Psychometrics calls violations of this assumption \\textit{differential item functioning} (DIF): an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly.\n\nWe apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) statistic \\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988differential} to characterize item-level behavioral stability in the METABENCH repository \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}---5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items. We treat MH strictly as a nonparametric $\\chi^2$ test for group$\\times$item interaction, without claiming latent-trait interpretation, since unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$; bifactor IRT also fails to converge with acceptable fit).\n\nOur central finding: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large behavioral heterogeneity between temporal cohorts. A permutation null decomposes this into $\\sim$16.4\\% attributable to cohort composition differences and $\\sim$14.9\\% that persists after controls. We characterize this heterogeneity without claiming psychometric interpretation---it is an empirical property of how item response patterns differ across model generations.\n\nThree further findings shape the interpretation: (1)~reasoning-RL models fundamentally destabilize DIF flags (Jaccard~$= 0.43$), showing that flags are cohort-dependent; (2)~three independent detection methods flag nearly disjoint item sets (pairwise Jaccard $0.11$--$0.19$), suggesting a construct validity challenge across the field; (3)~a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching provides principled infrastructure for evaluating any item-level detector, with known power limitations.\n\nWe frame item-level stability as an operational metric for benchmark maintenance: characterize, monitor, but do not remediate---removing 31\\% of items degrades reliability below acceptable thresholds.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.} \\citet{li2024task} and \\citet{oren2024proving} document web-overlap as a proxy for benchmark contamination. Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting} and ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} detect contamination via model-internal signals. DVD \\citep{oren2024proving} uses generation variance. All validate against web-overlap labels, which measure global exposure rather than item-level differential effects.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.} MH-DIF \\citep{holland1988differential} is the standard nonparametric method in educational testing. \\citet{sinharay2006bayesian} extend it to cumulative Bayesian frameworks. Item-Focused Trees \\citep{tutz2016item} treat covariates continuously. DIF has not previously been applied to LLM benchmark evaluation at scale.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Stability.} \\citet{raju2024growing} identify ``growing pains'' in LLM evaluation via anchor-item methods. Our DIF analysis complements their work by identifying \\emph{which} items are degrading. \\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} show benchmark redundancy enables subsampling---consistent with our finding that ranking stability is maintained despite 31\\% item instability.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF as Nonparametric Group-Comparison Test}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times I}$ and a grouping variable $G \\in \\{\\text{ref}, \\text{foc}\\}$, the MH statistic tests whether the odds of a correct response differ between groups after stratifying by total score. The common odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the ETS delta scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified as A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), or C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) per ETS guidelines. We report C-class percentage (C\\%) as the primary instability metric.\n\nWe use MH strictly as a nonparametric test. Total-score stratification controls for overall ability without requiring a latent-trait model. This is critical: MMLU's 57-subject structure makes unidimensional IRT misspecified (Section~\\ref{sec:irt_fit}), and we do not claim that MH conditioning fully absorbs all confounds.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\nTo validate detection power under controlled ground truth, we inject known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices. For selected ``clean'' items in the focal cohort, we flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses with probability $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ for a random subset of focal models, simulating contamination that differentially benefits newer models.\n\nThe key innovation is \\textbf{cross-subject matching}: when testing items from subject $s$, we use total score on all \\emph{other} subjects as the MH matching variable. This prevents the injected contamination from corrupting the conditioning variable. Detection power is measured as $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$_{\\text{injected}}$ $-$ TPR$_{\\text{baseline}}$, and false-positive control as $\\Delta$FPR.\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null}\n\nWe establish a null distribution for C\\% via 1,000 permutations: temporal labels (2023 vs.\\ 2024) are randomly reassigned while preserving group sizes, and MH-DIF is recomputed each time. The null mean C\\% quantifies how much behavioral heterogeneity arises from cohort composition alone (ability distribution, family structure, training vintage) rather than temporal signal.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\nWe partition the 5,227 METABENCH MMLU models into reference (2023, $N = 1{,}080$) and focal (2024-H1, $N = 807$) cohorts. MH-DIF identifies 31.3\\% of 12,508 items as C-class. A random-split sanity check yields C\\% $= 0.18\\%$, confirming the signal is not a statistical artifact.\n\nWithin-family analysis shows elevated C\\%: Llama-2$\\to$3 at 61.3\\% and Mistral$\\to$Mixtral at 38.7\\%. However, these comparisons confound temporal change with architecture, data, and training procedure changes. Domain-level accuracy improvement correlates weakly with DIF rates ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but this ecological correlation on $N = 57$ subjects has limited item-level interpretability. Removing 15.4\\% negative-discrimination items reduces C\\% by only 1.2pp.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Raw & After Perm.\\ Control & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% & 31.3\\% & 14.9\\% & Inflated by cohort comp. \\\\\nEnrichment vs.\\ Li & 0.96 & 0.96 & Trivially expected \\\\\nIRT misfit & 98\\% $|L_z|{>}2$ & N/A & MH conditioning unreliable \\\\\nDomain $\\rho$ & 0.307 & N/A & Ecol.\\ fallacy on $N{=}57$ \\\\\nWithin-family C\\% & 38.7--61.3\\% & N/A & Architecture confounded \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cohort definition $\\times$ item filtering sensitivity.}\n\\label{tab:cohort_filtering}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nCohort & Filter & C\\% & Effective C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & All items & 31.3 & 14.9 \\\\\nTemporal & No neg-disc & 30.1 & 13.7 \\\\\nAbility & All items & 2.9 & N/A \\\\\nAbility & No neg-disc & 2.7 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null Decomposition}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\nThe 1,000-iteration permutation null yields mean C\\% $= 16.4\\%$ (SD $= 2.1\\%$, 95\\% CI $[12.6, 20.3]$). The observed 31.3\\% exceeds this null ($z = 7.1$, $p < 10^{-12}$), so temporal signal exists beyond cohort composition. However, 52.4\\% of the raw C\\% is attributable to cohort composition effects---ability distribution shift ($d = -0.17$), model family structure differences, and training data vintage variation---that MH's total-score conditioning cannot fully absorb.\n\nThe random-split control (C\\% $= 0.18\\%$) is not contradictory: random splits preserve within-cohort structure, while permutation preserves between-cohort composition differences. The 16.4\\% null arises specifically because 2023 and 2024 models differ in ways beyond temporal signal.\n\nEffective C\\% after permutation control is 14.9\\%: this represents item-level behavioral change that cannot be attributed to cohort composition alone. Even this figure may include residual confounds (tokenizer changes, training data composition within families) not captured by the permutation procedure.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Permutation null distribution ($B = 1{,}000$).}\n\\label{tab:perm_null}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nNull mean C\\% & 16.4 \\\\\nNull SD & 2.1 \\\\\nNull 95\\% CI & $[12.6, 20.3]$ \\\\\nObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\n$z$-score & 7.1 \\\\\nEffective C\\% & 14.9 \\\\\nFraction spurious & 52.4\\% \\\\\nRandom-split C\\% & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Decomposition of observed C\\%.}\n\\label{tab:decomposition}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrl@{}}\n\\toprule\nSource & C\\% & Mechanism \\\\\n\\midrule\nGenuine temporal & 14.9 & Item-level behavioral change \\\\\nCohort composition & 16.4 & Ability + family + vintage \\\\\n\\midrule\nTotal & 31.3 & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Stability Profiles}\n\\label{sec:multi_bench}\n\nWe replicate temporal DIF on five additional METABENCH benchmarks (Table~\\ref{tab:multi_bench}). Raw C\\% ranges from 10.1\\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\\% (MMLU). After permutation controls, effective C\\% ranges from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\%. All random-split controls remain below 1\\%, confirming genuine between-cohort signal across benchmarks. MMLU's outlier status (14.9\\% effective) likely reflects its 57-subject structure providing more opportunities for heterogeneity. HellaSwag's low effective rate (2.3\\%) is consistent with its relatively homogeneous task structure (sentence completion).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Multi-benchmark DIF summary.}\n\\label{tab:multi_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & Raw C\\% & Perm.\\ Null & Eff.\\ C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12{,}508 & 31.3 & 16.4 & 14.9 \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 22.4 & 14.8 & 7.6 \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1{,}267 & 14.7 & 10.1 & 4.6 \\\\\nARC & 1{,}172 & 13.8 & 9.2 & 4.6 \\\\\nGSM8K & 1{,}319 & 12.4 & 8.7 & 3.7 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10{,}042 & 10.1 & 7.8 & 2.3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nGSM8K (1,319 items, $N_{\\text{ref}} = 1{,}017$, $N_{\\text{foc}} = 804$) shows raw temporal C\\% $= 12.4\\%$, with a permutation null of 8.7\\% yielding effective C\\% $= 3.7\\%$---near the noise floor. The lower rate likely reflects GSM8K's single-domain structure: math ability improvement maps more uniformly across items, and MH's total-score conditioning absorbs this effect more effectively.\n\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR on GSM8K is also reduced: 0.38 at $p = 0.3$ (standard injection) and 0.27 (external injection), reflecting lower power from the smaller item pool.\n\nWe attempted to correlate DIF flags with the GSM1K contamination gap \\citep{zhang2024careful} as external validation. On $N = 33$ matched models, the correlation is negative (Pearson $r = -0.29$, $p = 0.10$; Spearman $\\rho = -0.22$, $p = 0.21$; partial $r = -0.18$, $p = 0.32$) but non-significant, with confidence intervals crossing zero. At this sample size, we cannot draw conclusions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Raw C\\% & Perm.\\ Null & Eff.\\ C\\% & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3 & 16.4 & 14.9 & Moderate signal \\\\\nGSM8K & 12.4 & 8.7 & 3.7 & Near noise floor \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & 95\\% CI & $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.29$ & $[-0.58, 0.06]$ & 0.10 \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.22$ & $[-0.53, 0.14]$ & 0.21 \\\\\nPartial $r$ & $-0.18$ & $[-0.50, 0.19]$ & 0.32 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semi_synthetic}\n\nWe inject differential contamination into 150 of 500 selected clean items at four exploitation rates. Table~\\ref{tab:semi_synth} shows $\\Delta$TPR under the worst-case parameterization. At $p = 0.5$, $\\Delta$TPR $= 0.26$---moderate detection power, substantially below the registry's optimistic estimate of 0.508, because the high baseline FPR (0.318) compresses the available signal range.\n\nCross-subject matching remains effective: $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.003$ (Table~\\ref{tab:matching}), confirming that using scores from other subjects as the conditioning variable prevents contamination from corrupting the matching. Within-subject matching, by contrast, inflates FPR by 0.186.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR under worst-tier parameterization.}\n\\label{tab:semi_synth}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nInjection $p$ & TPR & Baseline FPR & $\\Delta$TPR & Registry $\\Delta$TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.3 & 0.39 & 0.318 & 0.07 & 0.222 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.58 & 0.318 & 0.26 & 0.508 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.74 & 0.318 & 0.42 & 0.664 \\\\\n1.0 & 0.87 & 0.318 & 0.55 & 0.674 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cross-subject vs.\\ within-subject matching.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & +0.186 & 0.34 & FPR inflated \\\\\nCross-subject & +0.003 & 0.26 & FPR stable \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness}\n\\label{sec:rl_robustness}\n\nAdding 13 reasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) to the focal cohort reduces C\\% from 31.3\\% to 24.1\\% and shifts 57\\% of individual item flags (Jaccard $= 0.43$ vs.\\ baseline; Table~\\ref{tab:rl_stability}). Alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO) are benign (Jaccard $= 0.82$). This demonstrates that DIF flags are cohort-dependent properties, not stable item characteristics. Our characterization is specific to the 2023-vs-2024 temporal split and should not be extrapolated to arbitrary cohort definitions.\n\nTraditional detectors show minimal sensitivity to RL composition: Min-K\\% AUROC drops 0.03 ($p = 0.34$, DeLong test), ConStat drops 0.03 ($p = 0.41$). TOST equivalence is borderline ($p = 0.08$). The power analysis boundary ($\\delta = 1.8$) with $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models is insufficient to support robust conclusions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF flag stability under cohort composition changes.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nComparison & Jaccard & C\\% shift & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nBaseline vs.\\ +align-RL & 0.82 & $-1.5$pp & Stable \\\\\nBaseline vs.\\ +reason-RL & 0.43 & $-7.2$pp & Unstable \\\\\nAlign-RL vs.\\ reason-RL & 0.48 & $-5.7$pp & RL type dominant \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Traditional detector AUROC comparison.}\n\\label{tab:trad_detectors}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nDetector & AUROC (base) & AUROC (RL) & $\\Delta$ & DeLong $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.71 & 0.68 & $-0.03$ & 0.34 \\\\\nConStat & 0.65 & 0.62 & $-0.03$ & 0.41 \\\\\nMH-DIF & N/A & N/A & Jacc.\\ 0.43 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Alternative Detection Methods}\n\\label{sec:alternatives}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat Perturbation Testing.} We generate reformulations for 237 MMLU items (91\\% semantic equivalence verified) and apply ConStat's FDR-controlled detection. ConStat achieves AUROC $= 0.72$ on non-RL models but degrades to 0.54 on reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:constat}). The overlap between ConStat and DIF flags is minimal (Jaccard $= 0.15$): ConStat identifies 47 items missed by DIF, while DIF flags 312 items missed by ConStat.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{ConStat detection performance by model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel type & $N$ & Det.\\ rate & AUROC & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nNon-RL & 50 & 0.24 & 0.72 & 0.06 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 30 & 0.18 & 0.64 & 0.07 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.09 & 0.54 & 0.05 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DVD Generation Variance.} We sample 50 responses per item at temperature 0.8 across 12 models on 500 items. DVD achieves AUROC $= 0.61$ with detection rate 0.18 and identifies 43 bimodal items (Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}). Overlap with DIF is even lower (Jaccard $= 0.11$), and with ConStat marginally higher (Jaccard $= 0.19$). The three-way disagreement---DIF, ConStat, and DVD flag nearly disjoint item sets---suggests each method measures a different construct, raising a field-wide validity concern.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DVD detection summary.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nAUROC & 0.61 \\\\\nDetection rate & 0.18 \\\\\nFPR & 0.08 \\\\\nBimodal items & 43 \\\\\nOverlap with DIF (Jaccard) & 0.11 \\\\\nOverlap with ConStat (Jaccard) & 0.19 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nWe attempted to fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD into a multi-signal ensemble. Pairwise signal correlations are extremely low (DIF--ConStat: 0.09, DIF--DVD: 0.06, ConStat--DVD: 0.13), indicating the methods do not measure the same underlying construct. Majority-vote AUROC ($0.58$) is lower than the best individual detector (ConStat: 0.72 on non-RL). Weighted fusion cross-validation shows weight instability across folds (sign flips). Only 4\\% of items are flagged by all three methods. We consider this experiment a negative result: ensemble fusion is not viable when constituent signals are effectively orthogonal.\n\n\\subsection{IRT Model Fit}\n\\label{sec:irt_fit}\n\nWe fit both unidimensional 2PL and bifactor (1 general + 57 specific factors) IRT models to the $12{,}508 \\times 5{,}219$ response matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:irt_fit}). The unidimensional 2PL converges but with poor fit (RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.68$). The bifactor model fails to converge after three attempts with different estimation methods; forced estimates yield RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.72$---still far below acceptable thresholds (RMSEA $< 0.06$, CFI $> 0.95$). With $> 25{,}000$ parameters and the 57-subject factor structure, the bifactor model is severely overparameterized relative to the sample. Anchor item selection for equating is impossible without reliable parameter estimates.\n\nThis confirms that IRT-based interpretation of MH-DIF results is not warranted for MMLU. Our use of MH as a nonparametric test does not require IRT model fit, but the inability to fit any IRT model means the total-score conditioning may not adequately control for ability differences.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IRT model fit comparison.}\n\\label{tab:irt_fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & SRMR & Converged \\\\\n\\midrule\nUnidim.\\ 2PL & 0.094 & 0.68 & 0.65 & 0.12 & Yes (poor) \\\\\nBifactor (57) & 0.089 & 0.72 & 0.69 & 0.11 & No (forced) \\\\\nAcceptable & ${<}0.06$ & ${>}0.95$ & ${>}0.95$ & ${<}0.08$ & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{sec:arch}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Removing the single non-decoder model changes nothing: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, IRT fit identical (Table~\\ref{tab:arch}). Architecture heterogeneity is not the source of IRT misfit; the real source is MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality combined with non-normal ability distributions.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Architecture stratification.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $N$ & C\\% & RMSEA & CFI \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.094 & 0.68 \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.094 & 0.68 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Regression Transparency}\n\\label{sec:regression}\n\nWe examine what predicts DIF flags via logistic regression (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}). Domain-level accuracy change alone explains almost nothing (McFadden $R^2 = 0.0007$). Adding item properties (difficulty, discrimination) raises $R^2$ to 0.0423---a 60$\\times$ increase, but still modest in absolute terms. A Simpson's paradox emerges: item accuracy change OR flips from 1.08 (bivariate) to 0.92 (controlling for item properties), meaning higher-accuracy-change items are \\textit{less} likely to show DIF after adjusting for difficulty and discrimination. Deviance analysis shows 96\\% of model variance comes from item properties, not accuracy change. DIF flags are more associated with item structural properties (harder, more discriminating items) than with temporal accuracy shifts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Logistic regression hierarchy.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n\\toprule\nModel & Predictors & McF.\\ $R^2$ & Key OR \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 [1.19--2.03] \\\\\nM2 & $+$ item props & 0.0423 & 0.73 ($\\Delta$acc flips) \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\nM4 & $+$ item props & 0.0434 & 0.92 ($\\Delta$acc flips) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Replacement}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5\\%--50\\%) to assess whether removing unstable items is a viable remediation strategy (Table~\\ref{tab:degradation}). Ranking stability ($\\rho$) degrades gracefully: 0.981 at 20\\% replacement. However, reliability (Cronbach's $\\alpha$) drops from 0.94 to 0.86 at 20\\%---approaching the educational testing minimum (0.85)---and to 0.74 at 50\\%, which is unacceptable. Construct coverage falls to 0.82 at 31\\% replacement. This demonstrates that item removal is not a viable response to instability at the 31\\% level.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Graceful degradation under item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nReplaced & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability $\\alpha$ & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% & 1.000 & 0.94 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.998 & 0.93 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.994 & 0.91 & 0.93 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.981 & 0.86 & 0.88 \\\\\n31\\% & 0.969 & 0.85 & 0.82 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.937 & 0.74 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs a robustness check, we apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring a binary cohort split. IFT flags 22.8\\% of items (vs.\\ MH's 31.3\\%), with moderate overlap (Jaccard $= 0.52$; Table~\\ref{tab:ift}). Split points concentrate around 2023-Q3 to 2024-Q1, broadly validating the temporal split. However, IFT itself is unstable: across random seeds, Jaccard $= 0.63$, indicating that the tree structure is sensitive to initialization. IFT detects 312 nonlinear DIF items (non-monotonic difficulty change over time) that MH cannot identify, though this may reflect noise rather than genuine nonlinear effects.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMethod & C\\% & Unique flags & Overlap (Jacc.) \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 31.3 & 1{,}583 & 0.52 \\\\\nIFT & 22.8 & 512 & 0.52 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Difficulty Calibration for Item Generation}\n\\label{sec:item_gen}\n\nWe generated 200 replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions (142 passing quality filters) and evaluated difficulty calibration via two methods (Table~\\ref{tab:calibration}). Both fail to reach the target correlation of 0.75: prompt-based targeting achieves $\\rho = 0.18$, student simulation reaches $\\rho = 0.41$, and the combination yields $\\rho = 0.47$. MMLU's multi-domain knowledge items make difficulty prediction substantially harder than for single-domain math benchmarks where similar methods achieve $r = 0.75$--$0.82$. Equating correlation ($\\rho = 0.88$) falls short of the 0.95 target. Item regeneration is not viable with current methods.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Difficulty calibration methods.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ & Target & Verdict \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based & 0.18 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\nStudent sim. & 0.41 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\nCombined & 0.47 & 0.75 & Fail \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors \\citep{sinharay2006bayesian} and simulate four successive administrations (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian_dif}). Cumulative power improves from 0.40 (single administration) to 0.61 (four administrations), but remains below the target of 0.70. The method assumes exchangeable examinee populations across administrations, which is violated in LLM benchmarks where successive cohorts are systematically stronger. Prior specification sensitivity is high (power ranges 0.48--0.64 across three prior choices), indicating that results depend on subjective modeling decisions. This remains a simulation-only exploration without real-data validation.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cumulative DIF power by administration count.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n$N_{\\text{admin}}$ & Power & Target & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.40 & 0.70 & $-0.30$ \\\\\n2 & 0.49 & 0.70 & $-0.21$ \\\\\n3 & 0.56 & 0.70 & $-0.14$ \\\\\n4 & 0.61 & 0.70 & $-0.09$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{The 31\\% Headline, Decomposed.} Our central finding is not that 31\\% of MMLU items are ``contaminated'' or ``broken,'' but that 31\\% exhibit behavioral heterogeneity between temporal cohorts. Approximately half (16.4\\%) is attributable to cohort composition differences that MH's total-score conditioning cannot absorb. The remaining $\\sim$15\\% represents genuine item-level behavioral change---substantial, but more modest than the raw headline suggests. Across benchmarks, effective rates range from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\%, making MMLU an outlier rather than representative.\n\n\\paragraph{Method Disagreement as Field-Wide Challenge.} The near-orthogonality of DIF, ConStat, and DVD flags (pairwise Jaccard $0.06$--$0.19$) is perhaps our most provocative finding. If three independent methods claiming to detect item-level anomalies flag almost entirely different items, the field faces a construct validity crisis: either these methods measure genuinely different phenomena, or at least some are measuring artifacts. The ensemble failure (majority-vote AUROC $= 0.58$) confirms that simple aggregation cannot resolve this disagreement.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort Dependence.} The RL robustness experiment (Jaccard $= 0.43$ when adding 13 reasoning-RL models) demonstrates that DIF flags are properties of the cohort definition, not stable item attributes. Our characterization is specific to the 2023-vs-2024 temporal split. Benchmark maintainers should not treat DIF results as fixed item labels but as cohort-specific diagnostics.\n\n\\paragraph{Monitoring Over Remediation.} Graceful degradation analysis shows that removing 31\\% of items would reduce reliability from 0.94 to $\\sim$0.85 and construct coverage to 0.82. Item generation cannot yet produce calibrated replacements ($\\rho = 0.47$ vs.\\ target 0.75). Together, these findings argue for monitoring stability over time rather than acting on individual flags.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n%==============================================================================\n\nFirst, MH's total-score conditioning is a limited ability proxy. With 98\\% IRT misfit and failed bifactor convergence, the conditioning may not adequately control for multidimensional ability differences between cohorts. Multidimensional IRT or propensity-score methods may yield different results.\n\nSecond, our characterization is cohort-specific. The 14.9\\% effective rate depends on the 2023-vs-2024 split; different temporal boundaries, different benchmarks, or the inclusion of reasoning-RL models produce substantially different results (effective C\\% ranges from 2.3\\% to 14.9\\% across benchmarks; Jaccard $= 0.43$ with RL models).\n\nThird, the semi-synthetic framework uses a simplified contamination mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping). Real contamination may involve subtler changes in response distributions that our injection does not capture. Detection power under worst-case parameterization is moderate ($\\Delta$TPR $= 0.26$ at $p = 0.5$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the first large-scale item-level stability analysis of LLM benchmarks, applying nonparametric MH analysis to 5,227 models across 12,508 MMLU items and five additional benchmarks. The observed 31\\% raw behavioral heterogeneity decomposes into approximately 15\\% genuine temporal signal and 16\\% cohort composition artifact. This signal is benchmark-specific (2--15\\% effective rate across benchmarks), method-dependent (three detectors produce near-disjoint flags), and cohort-sensitive (reasoning-RL models destabilize flags). We contribute a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $\\approx 0$) as reusable infrastructure. Our findings motivate treating item-level stability as an ongoing monitoring metric rather than a one-time diagnostic.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nJasper Dekoninck, Mark Niklas M{\\\"u}ller, and Martin Vechev.\n\\newblock Con{S}tat: Statistical testing for {LLM} contamination detection.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel-{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nNathan Mantel and William Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\textit{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et~al.(2024)]{oren2024proving}\nYonatan Oren, Nicole Meister, Niladri Chatterji, Faisal Ladhak, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black box language models.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nYotam Perlitz, Elron Bandel, Ariel Gera, Ofir Arviv, Liat Ein-Dor, Eyal Shnarch, Noam Slonim, Michal Shmueli-Scheuer, and Leshem Choshen.\n\\newblock Efficient benchmarking (of language models).\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.11696}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nFelipe~Maia Polo, Lucas Weber, Leshem Choshen, Yuekai Sun, Gongjun Xu, and Mikhail Yurochkin.\n\\newblock tiny{B}enchmarks: evaluating {LLM}s with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Raju et~al.(2024)]{raju2024growing}\nAnirudh Raju, Harish Kamath, and Heather Lent.\n\\newblock Growing pains: Understanding the limitations of {LLM} evaluation benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.08455}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nSandip Sinharay, Matthew~S. Johnson, and H.~Swaminathan.\n\\newblock Bayesian estimation of differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\textit{Psychometrika}, 71(1):145--168, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\textit{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{zhang2024careful}\nHugh Zhang, Jeff Da, Dean Lee, Vaughn Robinson, Catherine Wu, Will Song, Tiffany Zhao, Pranav Raja, Dylan Slack, Qin Lv, Sean Hendryx, Summer Yue, and others.\n\\newblock A careful examination of large language model performance on grade school math.\n\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.00332}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
